.TH CYAML_DICTIONARY_NEXT 3 "2026-09-29" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_dictionary_next \- step a dictionary cursor onto the next member
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

bool cyaml_dictionary_next(cyaml_dictionary_iter *it);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Steps the cursor it, which cyaml_dictionary_first(3) filled, onto the
member that follows the current one in insertion order.  After a call
that returns true, it->key and it->value describe that member.
The cursor holds that member from the moment it stepped onto the
current one, so it stays valid across every change that leaves that
successor in place: a replacement of any value, the removal of any
other member, and an insert.  Removing the successor or destroying the
dictionary invalidates it.  cyaml_dictionary_first(3) describes the
order, the lifetime of it->key and it->value, and each of those
changes.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
it  A cursor that cyaml_dictionary_first(3) filled.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
true when it now stands on the next member.

false when the previous call gave the last member, and on every call
after that; the function then sets it->key and it->value to NULL.
Also false when it is NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Removing the current member with cyaml_dictionary_remove(3) does not
invalidate the cursor; removing the successor or destroying the
dictionary does.  See
cyaml_dictionary_first(3) for every change and its effect.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_dictionary_first (3),
.BR cyaml_dictionary_remove (3)
